You can't use $r_s = \frac{2GM}{c^2}$, because that is for a Schwarzchild black hole. You want $r_s=\frac{2GM}{c^2}=\frac{8GM}{3c^2}$, so $M=\frac{8}{9} \frac{c^2}{G}$. Then $M=\frac{4\pi}{3}r^3\rho = \frac{8}{9}\frac{c^2}{G}$. So, $r=\sqrt[3]{\frac{9}{4\pi}\frac{c^2}{G}\rho}=\sqrt[3]{\frac{9}{4\pi}\frac{c^2}{G}\frac{6\, 10^{-3}}{m^3}}=\frac{9}{4\pi}\sqrt[3]{\frac{c^2}{G}\frac{6\, 10^{-3}}{m^3}}$. You should be able to finish from here. –  Manishearth Jul 10 '12 at 10:12
