 The radius of the event horizon for a black hole with mass M is given by

r_{S}=\left(2GM_{S}\right)^{1 / 2}

where G is the gravitational constant and M_{S} is the mass of the black hole in solar masses.

The Schwarzschild radius is given by

r_{S}=\left(2Gm_{S}\right)^{1 / 2}

where m_{S} is the mass of the black hole in solar masses. The Schwarzschild radius is the radius of the event horizon.

The number of photons per second emitted by a black hole with mass M is

